        "title":  "Architecting memory and storage in the AI era",
        "source":  "MIT Technology Review",
        "url":  "https://www.technologyreview.com/2026/09/04/1140872/architecting-memory-and-storage-in-the-ai-era/",
        "date":  "2026-09-04",
        "theme":  "Platforms",
        "summary":  "The article argues that AI inference workloads require enterprises to rearchitect data center infrastructure, treating memory and storage as strategic assets rather than supporting hardware. Data movement has become the primary bottleneck in AI workloads, necessitating integrated system design across compute, memory, storage, and networking to optimize efficiency and reduce latency in real-time AI applications.",
        "execSummary":  "The infrastructure argument here has a direct enterprise cost implication: organizations running AI inference on data center architectures designed for traditional compute will hit performance and cost walls that cannot be solved by adding more GPUs. Memory bandwidth and data movement — not raw compute — are now the binding constraints in AI workloads, which means infrastructure investment decisions made in the next 12-18 months will determine which organizations can scale AI economically. For enterprise leaders, the decision is whether to address this through on-premises rearchitecture, cloud-native AI inference services that abstract the infrastructure layer, or a hybrid model. The article implicitly favors integrated hardware solutions, which warrants scrutiny of potential vendor alignment.",
